sections.body:
You write Java in a `.java` text file. The `javac` compiler reads that source, checks it for syntax and type errors, and, if it's satisfied, writes out a `.class` file containing **bytecode**: a compact set of instructions for an abstract machine defined by the Java Virtual Machine specification. Compiling and running are separate steps, and one compiler rule will catch you on day one: a top-level `public` class must live in a source file named after that class plus `.java`. A file named `Main.java` containing `public class Hello { ... }` fails to compile with an error like `class Hello is public, should be declared in a file named Hello.java`, even though nothing inside the class is wrong.

```java
// File must be named Hello.java, not anything else,
// because the public class inside is named Hello.
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
```

A file may contain several classes, but only one of them may be `public`, and that one must match the file name. The rule is for `javac`'s benefit: when some other file mentions `Hello`, the compiler can go straight to `Hello.java` without scanning every source file. The output is a separate matter. `javac` always names each `.class` file after its class, whatever the source file was called, and that is the name the JVM's classloader looks up later.

Since Java 11 there is also a shortcut, `java Hello.java`, which compiles a single source file in memory and runs it without leaving a `.class` file behind. It's handy for small experiments. The two-step form is what build tools use, and it's what this lesson describes.

examples.code:
// Hello.java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}

// In a terminal:
// $ javac Hello.java      -> produces Hello.class (bytecode), prints nothing on success
// $ java Hello             -> loads Hello.class into the JVM and runs it
//                             prints: Hello, Java!

public class ErrorTiming {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 10;
        int divisor = 0;
        // int result = total / "oops";   // <- would NOT compile: incompatible types
        int result = total / divisor;      // <- compiles fine, throws ArithmeticException at RUN TIME
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

// Adder.java
public class Adder {
    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

// $ javac Adder.java
// $ javap -c Adder
// Compiled from "Adder.java"
// public class Adder {
//   public Adder();
//     Code:
//        0: aload_0
//        1: invokespecial #1   // Method java/lang/Object."<init>":()V
//        4: return
//
//   public static int add(int, int);
//     Code:
//        0: iload_0
//        1: iload_1
//        2: iadd
//        3: ireturn
// }

