public class CommandChecker {
    public static boolean isQuitCommand(String input) {
        String normalized = new String(input.trim());
        return normalized == "quit";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("quit"));       // expected true, prints false
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("  quit  "));    // expected true, prints false
    }
}

exercises.solutionCode:
public class CommandChecker {
    public static boolean isQuitCommand(String input) {
        String normalized = new String(input.trim());
        return normalized.equals("quit");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("quit"));        // true
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("  quit  "));     // true
    }
}

sections.body:
When you run `java Hello`, the launcher looks for this method inside the class you named: `public static void main(String[] args)`.

`public`: the JVM is calling this method from completely outside your class (it isn't one of your own objects invoking it), so the method must be accessible to outside callers; a `private` or package-private main cannot be found and invoked by something external to your code. `static`: when the program starts, no object of your class exists yet, so there is no instance to call an instance method on. A `static` method belongs to the class itself and can be called without constructing an object first. `void`: main does not hand a return value back to whatever called it, because 'whatever called it' is the JVM's own launcher, not ordinary Java code expecting to consume a return value; a program communicates a status back out to the operating system through a process exit code instead (covered further down), not through main's return type. `String[] args`: a required parameter slot for command-line arguments, always an array of strings, whether or not your program uses any arguments at all.

```java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
```

Get any one of these wrong (forget `static`, capitalize `Main`, use `int` instead of `void`) and the code still compiles. You have written a valid method that the launcher refuses to use as an entry point.

These are the rules through Java 24, including the Java 21 this course uses. Java 25 relaxed them (JEP 512): `main` may be an instance method, non-`public`, and take no parameters, so `void main() { ... }` is enough, and the JVM creates the object for you. The full traditional signature still works everywhere, and it is the one you'll meet in existing code.

Whatever you type after the class name on the command line becomes the contents of `args`, each piece as a `String` regardless of what it looks like. Your shell does the splitting, on whitespace, so a value containing spaces needs quotes: `java Greet "Alice Smith"` passes one argument. Running `java Greet Alice 30` gives `args = {"Alice", "30"}`. Note that `"30"` is the two-character string `"30"`, not the number 30; if your program needs it as a number, you must explicitly parse it, typically with `Integer.parseInt(args[1])`, which itself can throw `NumberFormatException` if the text isn't a valid number.

```java
public class Greet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Hello, stranger!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Hello, " + args[0] + "!");
        }
    }
}
```

If no arguments are supplied at all, `args` is not `null`; it's an array of length zero. Checking `args.length` before indexing into `args` is the standard defensive habit; accessing `args[0]` without checking length first, when no arguments were passed, throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` at run time, since the compiler has no way to know ahead of time how many arguments a user will type on the command line.

A Java program ends in one of three ways, each with a different resulting exit code (a small integer the operating system and shell scripts can inspect to know whether the program considered itself successful). Ordinary completion: `main` runs its last statement, or hits a bare `return;`, and there's nothing left to execute -- ends the process with exit code `0`, the conventional 'success' signal. (If your program started other non-daemon threads, such as a thread pool it never shut down, the JVM stays up until they finish. A program that 'hangs' after `main` returns usually has one of these.)

`System.exit(code)` shuts the JVM down from wherever it's called. No later statement in the caller runs, `finally` blocks are skipped, other threads are stopped, and only registered shutdown hooks get a chance to clean up. It reports whatever integer you pass as the process's exit code. Use it when a program must stop now with a particular status, such as a command-line tool signaling failure to a calling script. Avoid it in library code, where it would take down the application that called you.

```java
public class ExitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("no arguments supplied");
            System.exit(1);
        }
        System.out.println("this line never runs if args.length == 0");
    }
}
```

Finally, an exception that propagates all the way out of `main` without being caught anywhere terminates the program too: the JVM prints a stack trace to the standard error stream and exits with a nonzero status (conventionally `1`), signaling failure. An uncaught exception is no special crash mechanism: no method along the call chain handled it, so it reached the JVM.

examples.code:
public class Greet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Hello, stranger!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Hello, " + args[0] + "!");
        }
    }
}

// $ java Greet
//   -> Hello, stranger!
// $ java Greet Alice
//   -> Hello, Alice!

public class Broken {
    public void main(String[] args) {   // missing 'static'
        System.out.println("This will never print this way");
    }
}

// $ javac Broken.java     -> succeeds, no errors at all
// $ java Broken
// Error: Main method is not static in class Broken, please define the main method as:
//    public static void main(String[] args)
// (Java 21 output; Java 25 and later accept an instance main)

public class ExitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("about to check arguments");
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("no arguments supplied -- exiting with status 1");
            System.exit(1);
        }
        System.out.println("this only runs if an argument WAS supplied");
        int result = 10 / Integer.parseInt(args[0]);   // could throw ArithmeticException if args[0] is "0"
        System.out.println("result = " + result);
    }
}

